You have 3 threads:

Lecture13 object = l
        🔒
        │
 ┌──────┼──────────┐
 ↓      ↓          ↓
T-0    T-1        Main
 │      │           │
 │      │           │
data   own        wait
=1000  work         ⏳

Step-by-step flow:

1. Object is created:

Lecture13 l = new Lecture13();

There is one object:

l → 🔒 Lock
    data = 0

2. Thread-0 is created and started:

Runnable r = l::print;
Thread t = new Thread(r);
t.start();

Thread-0 executes:

public synchronized void print() {
    data = 100 * 10;
    notify();
}

Because print() is synchronized:

Thread-0
   ↓
enters print()
   ↓
gets l's lock 🔒
   ↓
data = 1000
   ↓
notify()

notify() wakes a thread waiting on l.

3. Thread-1 is created:

Thread t2 = new Thread(r2);
t2.start();

Thread-1 does:

System.out.println("I'm going to done my own work");

Thread-1 does not use l's lock.

So it can simply do its own work:

Thread-1
   ↓
"I'm going to done my own work"
   ↓
finished

It has nothing to do with wait()/notify() here.

4. Main thread reaches this:

synchronized (l) {
    System.out.println("Waiting for data");
    l.wait();
}

Main tries to get l's lock:

Main
  ↓
synchronized(l)
  ↓
gets l's lock 🔒
  ↓
prints "Waiting for data"
  ↓
l.wait()
  ↓
releases l's lock 🔓
  ↓
waits ⏳